If a clinician selects a potentially-sedating-medication for a patient then the clinician should consider a fall-risk for the patient.
If a clinician determines a potentially-sedating-medication-dosage for a patient then the clinician should consider a fall-risk for the patient.
A potentially-sedating-medication-example includes a tricyclic-antidepressant-example and includes an anticonvulsant-example and includes an opioid-example.
If a patient is an older-adult then every clinician should weigh an NSAID-benefit against an NSAID-risk for the patient during an NSAID-use-dose-and-duration-decision.
If a patient has a hypertension then every clinician should weigh an NSAID-benefit against an NSAID-risk for the patient during an NSAID-use-dose-and-duration-decision.
If a patient has a renal-insufficiency then every clinician should weigh an NSAID-benefit against an NSAID-risk for the patient during an NSAID-use-dose-and-duration-decision.
If a patient has a heart-failure then every clinician should weigh an NSAID-benefit against an NSAID-risk for the patient during an NSAID-use-dose-and-duration-decision.
If a patient has a peptic-ulcer-disease-risk then every clinician should weigh an NSAID-benefit against an NSAID-risk for the patient during an NSAID-use-dose-and-duration-decision.
If a patient has a cardiovascular-disease-risk then every clinician should weigh an NSAID-benefit against an NSAID-risk for the patient during an NSAID-use-dose-and-duration-decision.
